 "notes": [
  "Detected patterns (permutation importance): loss of perfectly flat areas (flat_ratio), cross-channel noise correlation in flat regions (channel_corr_smooth), share and discreteness of non-zero noise levels (res_nonzero, res_top2_mass), and how the classifier reacts to squeezes (brightness, JPEG).",
  "Round 4 changed only the training data: harmless Gaussian noise at sigma 0.005/0.02/0.04 with a fresh random pattern per image (round 3 had seen one fixed noise pattern), so the model learns attack noise rather than 'any noise that differs from the control'.",
  "Trade-off of round 4: fewer false alarms on noisy photos, but very weak UNSEEN attacks are caught less often (PGD 2/255: 88% -> 27%; FGSM 1/255: 17% -> 3.0%). Perturbations of 1-2 grey levels look like weak sensor noise to these features. Round 3 is kept as config/adversarial_model_iter3.joblib for rollback.",
  "Controlled simulation against a LOCAL ResNet18; not evidence of attacks on hosted VLMs; does not name the attack family. Adaptive attackers who know these features can evade them (Carlini & Wagner, 2017)."
 ]
